        <div id="how-enzymes-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Enzyme Cleaners Work (And What They Cannot Do)
          </h2>

          <p>
            An enzyme is a protein that speeds up a specific chemical reaction without being used up in the process. In a cleaning product, the enzymes are chosen because they break down the large organic molecules that make up stains and odors into smaller pieces that dissolve in water or are easily rinsed away. That is a very different job from what a detergent does. A detergent surrounds grease and lifts it off a surface. An enzyme actually takes the stain apart.
          </p>

          <p>
            Different enzymes target different kinds of mess, which is why labels often list several. Proteases break down proteins, the main component in blood, milk, egg, sweat and the protein fraction of urine and vomit. Amylases target starches such as those in food spills and some baby products. Lipases act on fats and oils, and cellulases can help with plant-based residue and fabric pilling. Urine adds a further complication, because dried urine leaves uric acid crystals that do not dissolve easily in water, and cleaners aimed at pets are designed with that problem in mind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smell is the reason most people buy these products, and it is worth understanding why enzymes help. Odor comes from small volatile molecules released by organic residue as bacteria feed on it. If you remove the smell with a fragrance or a neutralizer but leave the residue behind, the bacteria keep feeding and the odor returns, often on humid days when the moisture revives the residue. A product that digests the residue removes the food supply, which is the logic behind recommending enzymatic cleaners for pet urine.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are real limits. Enzymes are specialists and only act on the kinds of molecules they are built for, so they will not dissolve rust, mineral scale, paint or most grease from a workshop floor. They are also fragile. Heat, strong acids, strong alkalis and bleach can all reduce their activity, and they stop working when the surface dries out. Most of the disappointing results people report come from using the product like a conventional spray cleaner, when it has to be treated more like a slow, wet treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your smell comes from mold, mildew or a damp basement rather than pet or food residue, an enzyme cleaner is the wrong tool. Start with moisture control and a proper <a href="/best-mold-remover/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">mold remover</a>, and consider a <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">good air purifier</a> for what remains in the air.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
