        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do enzyme cleaners really work on pet urine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, when they are used as directed. Enzymes digest the organic compounds in urine instead of covering the smell, but they must stay wet and in contact with the residue. Fresh accidents respond best. Old stains that reached the carpet pad may need several saturating treatments or removal of the affected material.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an enzyme cleaner take to work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check the label, because times differ by product. Many directions call for leaving the area damp for several hours or letting it air dry fully, and heavily soiled spots can take a day or more to stop smelling. Rinsing, vacuuming or covering the spot early shortens the working time and reduces results.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an enzyme cleaner safe around pets and children?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most are marketed as pet-friendly once dry, but that depends on the product, so read the label. Keep pets and children away while the area is wet, ventilate the room, store the bottle where they cannot reach it, and call your vet or poison control if a pet drinks or licks the product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix an enzyme cleaner with bleach or vinegar?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Bleach and other strong disinfectants can deactivate enzymes, and mixing bleach with ammonia-based products creates toxic gas. Vinegar is acidic and can interfere with some formulas. Use one product at a time, rinse thoroughly between them, and follow the directions on the bottle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between enzymatic and bacterial cleaners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Enzymatic cleaners contain enzymes that break specific molecules down on contact. Bacterial cleaners add beneficial bacteria that consume organic residue over time and produce their own enzymes. Both need moisture and patience. Many pet products combine both approaches, so check the label if the distinction matters to you.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an enzyme cleaner in drains and laundry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some products are labelled for it, and some are not. Live-culture formulas often list laundry and drain maintenance on the label, while pet-specific sprays may only cover carpet and upholstery. Enzymes can help with organic odors in drains but will not clear a solid clog, so use a snake for that.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an enzyme cleaner damage carpet, wood or fabric?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Enzyme cleaners are generally gentler than harsh chemical cleaners, but dyes, natural fibres and finished wood can still react or swell with moisture. Always test a hidden spot, avoid over-wetting hardwood, and check the label for any surfaces the manufacturer says not to use it on.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
